==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|hƿæt}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/hwæt/|[ʍæt]|lang=ang}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*hwat}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*kʷos}}, {{m|ine-pro|*kʷod}}. Cognates include Old Frisian ''[[hwet]]'' (West Frisian {{m|fy|wat}}), Old Saxon ''[[hwat]]'' (Low German {{m|nds|wat}}), Dutch {{m|nl|wat}}, Old High German ''[[hwaz]]'' (German {{m|de|was}}), Old Norse ''[[hvat]]'' (Danish {{m|da|hvad}}, Swedish {{m|sv|vad}}), Gothic [[𐍈𐌰]]. The Indo-European root also led to the Latin {{m|la|quod}}, Lithuanian {{m|lt|kàd}}, and Irish {{m|ga|cad}}.

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|g=n}}

# [[what]]
# [[anything]]

=====Quotations=====
* Hwæt! Wē Gār-Dena﻿in ġēar-dagum þēod-cyninga ﻿þrymm ġefrūġnon, hū þā æþelingas ﻿ellen fremedon.
*: Listǃ We, of the [[gar]]-danes, in the [[days of yore]], have heard of [[thede]]-kings' glory, how those [[athelings]] did [[ellen]]-deeds^.
*^ '''ellen-deads''' = deeds of [[ellen]]; valorous deeds.

